{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-106647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y>9$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=9.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>9$ for all $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-106647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(9-y)"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9)"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5({y}^{2}-9)"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5y(y+9)"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5{y}^{2}(y-9)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y>8$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-22(y-8).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=22,$ and $a=8.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>8$ for all $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-106651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176+22y"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176y-22{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=22{y}^{2}-176"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y>30$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-28(y-30).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=28,$ and $a=30.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>30$ for all $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-106654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=28{y}^{2}-840"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840+28y"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840y-28{y}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y>20$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=80,$ and $a=20.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>20$ for all $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-105898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(20-y)"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20)"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80y(20-y)"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80(y+20)"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80y(y+20)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=2,a=500,$ and ${y}_{0}=600.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=400<a=500.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-105890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=400"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=600"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-105890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A container of water at a temperature of ${90}^{∘}\\text{C}$ is placed in a room. The temperature $T(t)$ of the water decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the water temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-106935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-19k(T-90)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90(T-19)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90k(T-19)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-90)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-37365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{∘}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-37365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-37365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-37365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-37365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-100(T-64)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-64(T-100)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-100)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-164)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-37365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A turkey reaches ${145}^{∘}\\text{F}$ in the oven. After removing the turkey from the oven, the turkey's temperature $T(t)$ decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the turkey? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" 