$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-21).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-106878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+21)"] ["math" "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)"] ["math" "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-21)"] ["math" "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T+21)"] ["math" "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-21)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a restaurant kitchen, a pot of soup reaches a temperature of ${200}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning off the stove, the temperature $T(t)$ of the soup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the kitchen temperature is ${76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the pot temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-106934" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106934" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-200(T-76)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-76k(T-200)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-200)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=200k(T-76)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-106934" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${60}^{∘}C.$ After $10$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${52}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${40}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${28}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={28}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${60}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=60,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 60 & =28+K \\\\ K & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+32{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${52}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(10)=52,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =28+32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ 24 & =32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-10k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${40}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =40 \\\\ 28+32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =40 \\\\ 32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =12 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${40}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈34\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-107125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "23\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "28\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "31\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "34\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "45\\,\\text{min}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "34\\,\\text{min}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh-baked cake can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the cake is ${340}^{∘}F,$ calculate the temperature of the cake after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that the temperature of the fresh-baked cake is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-70| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-70>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-70 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-70 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-70 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =70+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the cake was ${340}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=340,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}340 & =70+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 340 & =70+K \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=70+270{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the cake after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=70+270{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 8}≈{201}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-107120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{189}^{∘}\\text{F}"] ["math" "{194}^{∘}\\text{F}"] ["math" "{198}^{∘}\\text{F}"] ["math" "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}"] ["math" "{223}^{∘}\\text{F}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-15589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${25}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-15589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=90,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =25+75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 65 & =75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{13}{15} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the water has a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 25+75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the temperature of water will be ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15})}≈95\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-15589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-15589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-15589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "67\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "87\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "89\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "95\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "98\\,\\text{min}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-15589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "95\\,\\text{min}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${140}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $5$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-0.1\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-\\int 0.1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-0.1t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-0.1t+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-0.1t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${140}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =28+K \\\\ K & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+112{e}^{-0.1t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=5$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(5)=28+112{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 5}≈{96}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-107118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{76}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{92}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a compressor of a refrigerator can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the compressor is ${65}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the compressor after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that the temperature of the compressor of a refrigerator is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.12\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.12t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.12t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the compressor was ${65}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=65,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 65 & =21+K \\\\ K & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+44{e}^{-0.12t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the compressor after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=21+44{e}^{-0.12\\cdot 8}≈{38}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a15eb7ee50f5ea044f99c0a686d5da07bf707bacaec135b2f2df9b06c0e0a7d"} {"question" "q-107129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{29}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{31}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{43}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{58}^{∘}\\text{C}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "q-107129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/state.json" "file_sha256" "82dce49caf9e9bc868656e16d20cc7be20e80a1c6d19e4742c2b1f9e1179380e"} {"question" "e-8957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/example-8957.json" "file_sha256" "7e20277e1ccded44454af0e5287bec5fa28886b5c6de16e9613393f95e7da596"} {"question" "e-8957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/example-8957.json" "file_sha256" "7e20277e1ccded44454af0e5287bec5fa28886b5c6de16e9613393f95e7da596"} {"question" "e-6676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/example-6676.json" "file_sha256" "c43ff5a8e6c3a0f9b786e988b930f9d0fad8ad43eda5c86982507bc22c45b645"} {"question" "e-6676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/example-6676.json" "file_sha256" "c43ff5a8e6c3a0f9b786e988b930f9d0fad8ad43eda5c86982507bc22c45b645"} {"question" "e-6677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/example-6677.json" "file_sha256" "91afa29134a5eaece0d6e391e7cd4cca6d1f13d8957411cdd326ec13113f566d"} {"question" "e-6677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043944/example-6677.json" "file_sha256" "91afa29134a5eaece0d6e391e7cd4cca6d1f13d8957411cdd326ec13113f566d"}] "usage" {"e-6677" [] "e-8957" [] "q-37365" [] "q-105890" [] "e-6676" [] "q-105898" []} "basis" 1635 "retractions" [[17592186078625 :question/problem "Given that $y > 20$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?"] [17592186078625 :question/worked-solution "The inhibited decay form is $y^{\\prime}=-k(y-a)$ with $k>0$ and $y>a$. Here $-80(y-20)$ has $k=80$ and $a=20$. For $y>20$ the derivative is negative, so the solution decreases toward $20$."] [17592186078623 :question/worked-solution "Inhibited decay has the form $y^{\\prime}=-k(y-a)$ with $k>0$ and $y(0)>a$. The equation $y^{\\prime}=-2(y-500)$ has equilibrium $a=500$, and $y(0)=600>500$. Indeed, its solution is $y(t)=500+100e^{-2t}$, which decreases toward $500$."] [17592186078623 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186078798 :question/problem "A medical doctor records the temperature of $100\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $64\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{F}$, which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality."] [17592186078798 :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling uses the difference between the thermometer temperature and the room temperature: $T-64$. Therefore $\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-64)$ with $k>0$. The initial reading gives $T(0)=100$; it does not replace the ambient temperature in the differential equation."] [17592186078798 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186078295 :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$"] [17592186078295 :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0$.\n\nFrom the given options, 2 is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50)\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12$, and $a=50$. Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0$."] [17592186078261 :question/problem "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality."] [17592186078261 :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S)\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nT_S = 25\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25)\n$$"] [17592186078262 :question/problem "The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -0.25(T-23)$\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer."] [17592186078262 :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dT}{T - 23} &= -0.25dt \\\\\n\\int \\frac{dT}{T - 23} &= - \\int 0.25dt \\\\\n\\ln \\mid T - 23 \\mid &= -0.25t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0$.\nNext, we solve for $T(t)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT - 23 &= e^{-0.25t + C} \\\\\nT - 23 &= e^{C}e^{-0.25t} \\\\\nT - 23 &= Ke^{-0.25t} \\\\\nT &= 23 + Ke^{-0.25t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80$, and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n80 &= 23 + Ke^{0} \\\\\n80 &= 23 + K \\\\\nK &= 57\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186075048 17592186075049 17592186075050 17592186075051 17592186075052 17592186075060 17592186075061 17592186075062 17592186075063 17592186075064 17592186076000 17592186076001 17592186076002 17592186076003 17592186076004] "immutable_fields" [[17592186075047 :answer-field/correct] [17592186075047 :answer-field/id] [17592186075047 :answer-field/key] [17592186075047 :answer-field/type] [17592186075059 :answer-field/correct] [17592186075059 :answer-field/id] [17592186075059 :answer-field/key] [17592186075059 :answer-field/type] [17592186075999 :answer-field/correct] [17592186075999 :answer-field/id] [17592186075999 :answer-field/key] [17592186075999 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
